  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...There continues to be an elevated risk for severe \nthunderstorms and flash flooding today and Wednesday, with the \ngreater risk through this evening.\n\nA Tornado Watch has been issued until 9PM tonight for New \nJersey, eastern PA, and northern Delaware. \n\nA Severe Thunderstorm Watch has been issued until 10PM tonight\nfor the central and southern Delmarva.\n\nSevere thunderstorms continue to pop off across the region this\nafternoon and are expected to become more widespread through the\nevening. Several moving parts are coinciding to develop a\nwidespread severe and flash flooding threat. On the mesoscale,\nan MCS that developed across the Ohio River Valley earlier this\nmorning has collided with a warm front that has draped across\nthe NJ/NY border. This MCS has already resulted in several\ntornado warnings across northern NJ and into portions of\nsouthern NY and northeast PA. This threat looks to persist for\nat least the next few hours. On the synoptic scale, an upper-\nlevel trough continues to provide strong mid to upper level flow\nacross the Mid Atlantic, resulting in widespread convective\ndevelopment. In the near-term, the convection has begun to\nconcentrate itself along a pre-frontal trough across central and\neastern PA this afternoon. Storms have shown severe potential\ncross this region and have also begun to congeal and train,\nincreasing the flash flood threat. Another round of storms may \npivot from central PA/MD border down into the Delmarva tonight \nas well. Storm evolution has already become rather chaotic this \nafternoon and is expected to become more disorderly through the \nevening before the cold front arrives from the west and pushes\nall the convection towards the coast by midnight or so. Any of\nthese storms have the potential to also produce rainfall rates\nof 1-3 inches per hour at times. Overall rainfall amounts will\nbe a widespread 1-3 inches with locally higher amounts. Damaging\nwind gusts, flash flooding, a few tornadoes, and hail are all \npossible through tonight. \n\nDuring the overnight hours, convection may persist mainly across\nthe southern Delmarva as the frontal boundary stalls across the\nregion. The flash flood guidance for this region is not as\nsensitive to excessive rainfall, but will still bear monitoring\nthrough the early morning hours. \n\nOn Wednesday, the cold front will sag farther south and east and \ndepending on how quickly this clears our area there will be some \nshowers and thunderstorms, especially as additional shortwaves move \nthrough the flow within the upper-level trough overhead. This looks \nto be more concentrated across southern New Jersey to Delmarva but \ncould extend into the I-95 corridor for a time. There is some severe \nweather risk mainly near and south/east of the I-95 corridor with \nlocally damaging wind gusts the main threat. The greater instability \nand shear looks to reside across Delmarva on southward, where a \nlittle higher severe thunderstorm risk is indicated. Some locally \nexcessive rainfall is possible with some local flash flood risk, \nhowever this looks to be less compared to today given the shift \nfarther south and more into the coastal plain. Once the front moves \naway by later Wednesday, it turns drier for Thursday/Friday and \nright into the weekend.",
